<section class="work-directions page-shell" aria-labelledby="directions-heading">
  <div class="section-heading section-heading--stacked">
    <p class="eyebrow">Where we're working</p>
    <h2 id="directions-heading">Problems worth solving, systems worth understanding.</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">Five areas of work, each with its own requirements and engineering decisions.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="discipline-list">
    <article class="discipline-item">
      <span class="discipline-number">01</span>
      <div><h3>Intelligent systems</h3><p>A capable model still needs context, tools, permissions, and an honest account of what it has done. ForgeWire brings configurable models and capabilities into a workspace a person can follow and direct, without tying the whole application to a single provider or conversation.</p><p class="discipline-evidence"><strong>Our work:</strong> ForgeWire is in private development; its design and selected interfaces are documented in the <a href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/ForgeWire-Overview">public overview</a>.</p></div>
    </article>
    <article class="discipline-item">
      <span class="discipline-number">02</span>
      <div><h3>Distributed computing and execution infrastructure</h3><p>Moving work to another machine raises questions a model cannot answer for itself. Which runner is trusted? What can it do? What happens when it disconnects? Fabric is our separate control-plane effort for authenticated, policy-gated remote execution.</p><p class="discipline-evidence"><strong>Our work:</strong> Fabric remains in private development, with configuration-dependent integration for selected ForgeWire workflows.</p></div>
    </article>
    <article class="discipline-item">
      <span class="discipline-number">03</span>
      <div><h3>Developer and repository infrastructure</h3><p>Code survives a handoff more reliably than the decisions behind it. RepoPact keeps intent, authority, work items, constraints, and evidence with the source, so the next person or agent can continue without inventing missing history.</p><p class="discipline-evidence"><strong>Available software:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/repopact-core">RepoPact Core</a> is a released CLI package backed by a Rust semantic engine. Workbench remains a separate source-build preview.</p></div>
    </article>
    <article class="discipline-item">
      <span class="discipline-number">04</span>
      <div><h3>Systems integration and local software</h3><p>A useful application should own its responsibilities instead of outsourcing its identity to a provider or another product. Anvil handles local planning state. Flink handles communication, coordination, and human decision boundaries.</p><p class="discipline-evidence"><strong>Available software:</strong> Anvil is a developer preview. Flink has a published installer under its former ForgeLink name.</p></div>
    </article>
    <article class="discipline-item">
      <span class="discipline-number">05</span>
      <div><h3>Applied technical education</h3><p>Learning takes a sequence, practice, feedback, and a way to return to unfinished work. SkillForge Academy brings certification study and practical training into a local desktop application.</p><p class="discipline-evidence"><strong>Available software:</strong> SkillForge Academy has a published Windows application. Our newer course-platform work is in development and is not part of that release.</p></div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="work-principles" aria-labelledby="principles-heading">
  <div class="page-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Working principles</p>
    <h2 id="principles-heading">How we make decisions.</h2>
    <div class="principle-grid">
      <article><span>01</span><h3>Use what fits</h3><p>We use existing tools and libraries when they solve the problem well. We build our own when ownership, cost, constraints, or integration debt justify it.</p></article>
      <article><span>02</span><h3>Keep authority visible</h3><p>A tool requesting an action is not the same as being authorized to take it. We design around that distinction.</p></article>
      <article><span>03</span><h3>Leave a record</h3><p>If work is worth relying on, someone should be able to inspect the source, decisions, tests, or observed results behind it.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="principle-note">Different software, same responsibility to understand what it does and where its limits are.</p>
